  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the heart of the Estéron gorges, the Clue de Saint-Auban reveals a wide pool with jade-green reflections, framed by steep rock walls and the ruins of an old mill that recall the site's industrial past.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to Randoxygène (Alpes-Maritimes department), the scenic road overlooking the clue lets you see all of it, and access to the Estéron riverbed is on foot in about 5 minutes from the entrance of the clue, via the road and then sloping slabs. The site mainly attracts canyoners: the descent is only possible at low water, from 1 April to 31 October, on Mondays, Wednesdays, Fridays and Sundays from 9am to 5pm — a sign of the risks tied to the Estéron's flow. Randoxygène describes a succession of drops (up to 20 m) and pools, several with dangerous whirlpools, with abseils made outside the water flow because of its volume: this is not a walk for a dog. No specific information on dogs was found for this site: apply a leash by default, as everywhere out in nature in the department, and keep your dog out of the riverbed. On foot and without entering the water, the balcony road above the gorges gives a view of the clue; Randoxygène also describes the "Tracastel circuit" (a footpath from Saint-Auban, about 3 hours and 380 m of ascent, recommended from May to November) which returns along the clue road.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: stay alert to any unusual movement of water in the pools, even during an ordinary swim — flash floods on the Estéron can rise very quickly after a storm upstream, sometimes without any visible rain on site.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Clue d'Aiglun, a little further south in the same Estéron valley, is another Estéron site, mostly visited by canyoners (see the dedicated listing).</p>
